+  "problem": "\ud83e\udde0 **ARCHI (BARCHI-2)** \u2014 DISCOVERED on my own family's controls: C3 containment is **one-sided**, so an over-broad `expected_surfaces` silently widens the C7 delegation ceiling\n\nFound while correcting WP-2026-0112's own record, which declared a surface it does not touch. The declaration was wrong and **nothing in the controls would have caught it.**\n\n## The gap, conclusive by inspection\n`scripts/badf_gate.py:3455`:\n```python\nunexpected = [n for n in names if declared and not any(_surface_match(n, pat) for pat in patterns)]\n```\nThe set difference is computed in **one direction only**: for each *actually changed* path, is it covered by a declared pattern. No pass asks the inverse \u2014 *does this declared pattern match anything the work actually changed?* C3 therefore catches **over-reach** (changed but undeclared) and is structurally blind to **over-declaration** (declared but untouched).\n\n## Why that is not cosmetic\n`expected_surfaces` is not only a scope description \u2014 it is the **ceiling for delegated authority**. `badf_gate.py:1619`:\n```python\nif not any(_surface_match(probe, pat) for pat in surface):\n    raise ValidationError(f\"... delegation {name} allows path {ap!r} outside the work package's expected_surfaces ...\")\n```\nSo the invariant chain is `delegated \u2286 expected` (C7) and `actual \u2286 expected` (C3) \u2014 and **nothing binds `expected` to `actual`**. `expected_surfaces` can be arbitrarily wide and both controls still pass, while every extra pattern hands a delegate real authority over paths the work never needed. That is the proxy-vs-property class again, on a control I own: the declared surface is used as a proxy for the work's scope with nothing holding it to it.\n\n## Exposure, measured honestly \u2014 this is not a live incident\n```\nlanded WPs carrying BOTH a landed_as and expected_surfaces .... 1\n  of those, declaring >=1 surface they never touched ........... 0\n```\nOnly one work package has ever landed under this control (WP-2026-0109, the first controller run), and it is clean. **I am not claiming a fleet of over-declared packages exists** \u2014 the historical exposure is essentially nil, because `badf-build` went ACTIVE hours ago. The argument for fixing it now is the opposite of an incident report: exposure grows from here, and the cheapest moment to close a one-sided check is before it has history to grandfather.\n\n## Not proposing the remedy in this issue\nThe obvious shape \u2014 refuse or HOLD when a declared pattern matches zero changed paths \u2014 has a real design question behind it: a `discovery_allowance` pattern is *supposed* to match nothing in the ordinary case, and legitimately-anticipated-but-unused surfaces may deserve a warning rather than a refusal. That is a control-design decision for `badf-build`'s next rung, with its own red-first test, not something to bolt onto an in-flight test fix.\n\n**Out of scope for #228 / #229 / #230 / #231.** Filed so it does not evaporate.\n\n<!-- agent: architect -->\n",
